WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.253
Now the main idea.

00:00:01.253 --> 00:00:05.080
Each stringer is small compared with the whole section.

00:00:05.667 --> 00:00:09.127
So the bending stress across one stringer hardly changes.

00:00:09.127 --> 00:00:10.827
We can treat it as constant.

00:00:11.467 --> 00:00:19.651
That means we can replace the stringer by a single point, with the same area, at the same place.

00:00:19.651 --> 00:00:21.867
We call that point a boom.

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:30.420
Here's the key assumption of this whole topic: the booms carry all of the direct stress, and the skin between them carries only shear.

00:00:31.067 --> 00:00:38.640
With that, the bending part of the problem only needs the boom areas and where they are.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.147
I x x is just each boom area times its height squared, added up.

00:00:46.532 --> 00:00:50.741
Sometimes the skin does carry real direct stress.

00:00:50.741 --> 00:00:55.292
Then we give its share to the booms at its two edges.

00:00:56.632 --> 00:01:04.152
Say the stress is sigma one at boom one and sigma two at boom two, changing in a straight line between them.

00:01:04.432 --> 00:01:10.992
The two booms must do exactly what the panel did: give the same total force, and the same moment.

00:01:13.165 --> 00:01:15.839
To find B one, take moments about boom two.

00:01:15.839 --> 00:01:18.885
Split the stress into a rectangle and a triangle.

00:01:20.932 --> 00:01:25.025
The rectangle's force is sigma two times t D times b.

00:01:25.025 --> 00:01:28.732
It acts at the middle, b over two from boom two.

00:01:29.032 --> 00:01:39.683
The triangle's force is one half times sigma one minus sigma two, times t D times b.

00:01:39.683 --> 00:01:48.432
It acts two thirds of the way across, two b over three from boom two.

00:01:48.732 --> 00:01:55.612
Their moments add up to the moment of the boom force, sigma one B one, times b.

00:01:57.332 --> 00:02:04.172
Divide both sides by sigma one times b, and collect terms.

00:02:04.172 --> 00:02:13.252
You get B one equals t D b over six, times two plus sigma two over sigma one.

00:02:14.532 --> 00:02:21.892
The boom at the other end, B two, uses the same formula with one and two swapped: two plus sigma one over sigma two.

00:02:23.865 --> 00:02:29.438
So the boom area depends on the ratio of the two stresses.

00:02:29.438 --> 00:02:32.705
Two cases come up again and again.

00:02:33.032 --> 00:02:35.411
First case: a top skin, in bending.

00:02:35.411 --> 00:02:40.237
Both of its edges are at the same height, so they have the same stress.

00:02:40.237 --> 00:02:41.392
The ratio is one.

00:02:42.565 --> 00:02:48.839
Two plus one is three, and three over six is one half.

00:02:48.839 --> 00:02:52.325
Each boom gets t D b over two.

00:02:52.632 --> 00:03:00.991
Second case: a web that crosses the neutral axis, with its ends equally far above and below.

00:03:00.991 --> 00:03:04.262
The stresses are equal and opposite.

00:03:04.262 --> 00:03:06.352
The ratio is minus one.

00:03:07.332 --> 00:03:09.432
Two minus one is one.

00:03:09.432 --> 00:03:12.932
Each boom gets only t D b over six.

00:03:13.265 --> 00:03:13.910
Your turn.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:17.585
Pause here and do checkpoint two point one in your guide.

00:03:20.832 --> 00:03:25.491
Ten times three hundred, over six, is five hundred.

00:03:25.491 --> 00:03:30.149
So five hundred square millimeters go to each boom.

00:03:30.149 --> 00:03:33.712
We'll use exactly this in the homework.
